  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe proposed approach combines constrained optimization with systematic perturbations to explore the solution space more thoroughly. Instead of relying solely on local optimization or genetic algorithms, we use multiple initializations and perturbations to escape local optima while leveraging the efficiency of sequential quadratic programming (SQP). This approach addresses the non-convexity and combinatorial nature of the problem by maintaining diversity in the search process. The key idea is to generate and optimize multiple candidate solutions from different starting points, ensuring a more comprehensive exploration of the feasible region. This method balances global exploration with local refinement, aiming to find a solution closer to the global minimum.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points), dx = 2.0/n_points  \n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 (convolution(h, 1-h)[k] * dx)  \n- Constraints:  \n  1. h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] \u2200i  \n  2. \u2211h = n_points / 2  \n\nThe convolution is computed as a linear cross-correlation between h and (1-h), considering all possible shifts k. The objective function is evaluated by finding the maximum value across all lags, scaled by dx.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nThe algorithm proceeds in three stages: initialization, perturbation and optimization, and resolution refinement.  \n\n**Stage 1: Initialization**  \n1. If `initial_h_values` exists:  \n   a. Use it as the initial candidate.  \n   b. Generate 20 additional random candidates by sampling from a Dirichlet distribution scaled to meet \u2211h = n_points/2.  \n2. Else:  \n   a. Start with 50 uniformly random candidates scaled to meet the sum constraint.  \n3. Evaluate C\u2085 for all candidates.  \n4. Track the best solution (lowest C\u2085).  \n\n**Stage 2: Perturbation and Optimization**  \n1. For each candidate in the population:  \n   a. Apply a perturbation:  \n      - Randomly select 20% of the elements and add Gaussian noise (mean=0, std=0.1).  \n      - Clip to [0,1].  \n      - Rescale to ensure \u2211h = n_points/2.  \n   b. Run SQP with bounds and constraints to refine the candidate.  \n   c. Replace the original candidate if the perturbed solution improves C\u2085.  \n2. Repeat this process for up to 100 iterations or until no improvement is seen in 20 iterations.  \n\n**Stage 3: Resolution Refinement**  \n1. Start with the best solution found.  \n2. Double the resolution (n_points) and interpolate h.  \n3. Project to feasible region: clip and rescale.  \n4. Re-optimize at the new resolution using SQP.  \n5. Repeat refinement until the resolution reaches 1000 (or budget exhausted).  \n\n**Constraint Handling**  \nAfter any perturbation or optimization step:  \n- Clip h to [0,1].  \n- Rescale h to ensure \u2211h = n_points/2.  \n- This projection ensures feasibility without violating constraints.  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n- Population size: 50  \n- Perturbation rate: 20%  \n- SQP iterations per candidate: 50  \n- Resolution refinement factor: 2x per step  \n- Interpolation method: Linear  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n- Initial population evaluation: ~2 seconds  \n- Each perturbation+SQP cycle: ~0.5 seconds per candidate  \n- Total cycles: 100  \n- Expected total time: ~500 seconds, leaving ~100s for refinement  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n- Final check: Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and \u2211h = n_points/2  \n- Verify C\u2085 with `evaluate_erdos_solution`  \n- Return the best solution, its C\u2085 value, and resolution  \n\nFallback mechanism: If any step generates an invalid h, use the projection step to repair it and continue.",
